.TH DAQ-TUNNELS.SH 1 "2026-09-10" "mu2edaq-shifter-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
daq-tunnels.sh \- manage the SSH port forwards for the Mu2e shifter GUIs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B daq-tunnels.sh
[\fIoptions\fR]
{\fBstart\fR|\fBstop\fR|\fBrestart\fR|\fBstatus\fR}
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B daq-tunnels.sh
opens the local port forwards through which the otsdaq gateway, the DQM
displays, the subsystem GUIs and Grafana are reached from a shifter
workstation.
.PP
Two forwarding groups are opened, each in its own \fBssh\fR process:
.TP
.B daq
The otsdaq gateway, DQM and subsystem GUIs on \fImu2e\-cfo\-01\fR and
\fImu2e\-dl\-01\fR.
.TP
.B extra
Grafana on \fImu2e\-dcs\-01\fR.
.PP
Keeping them separate means a single unavailable forward, which
\fBExitOnForwardFailure\fR turns into a dead ssh, cannot take the other
group down with it.
.PP
Kerberos is used for authentication (\fBssh \-K\fR), so a valid ticket
is required; \fBstart\fR refuses to run without one.
.SS Stopping tunnels
The pid of each ssh process is recorded in a state file, and
\fBstop\fR terminates exactly those pids.
.PP
This is deliberate. An earlier version of this script stopped tunnels
with \fBpkill \-f 'ssh.*mu2e\-'\fR, which also matched \(em and killed
\(em the operator's own interactive ssh sessions to any Mu2e host, and
any unrelated tunnel to a Mu2e machine. The pattern-matching behaviour
is still available through \fB\-\-all\-matching\fR for the case where
the state file has been lost, but it is no longer the default.
.SS Service tables
The forwarded ports are defined by two tables of
\fIlabel\fB=\fIport\fR[\fB!\fR]\fB:\fIhost\fR entries. A \fB!\fR after
the port means it is fixed and takes no offset; every other port has
the value of \fB\-\-offset\fR added to it. Both tables can be replaced
from the environment if the ports move, without editing the script.
.PP
The defaults are:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shift=3075  crv=3085  calo=3025  trig=3045  dqm=5029
dqmvis=5033 crvdqm=8877! (mu2e-dl-01)     cfo=3095
stm=30351   trk=3065    grafana=3000!     (mu2e-dcs-01)
.fi
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
Configuration precedence is command line, then environment variable,
then .env file, then built-in default.
.TP
.BR start | stop | restart | status
The action to take. Defaults to \fBstart\fR.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-offset " " \fIN\fR
Add \fIN\fR to every non-fixed port, so a second operator can forward
the same services without colliding. Environment:
\fBMU2EDAQ_TUNNEL_OFFSET\fR. Default: \fI0\fR. A bare number is also
accepted as a positional argument, as in the original script.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSER\fR
SSH user, used for both the jump host and the target. Environment:
\fBMU2EDAQ_TUNNEL_USER\fR. Default: \fImu2eshift\fR.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-host " " \fIHOST\fR
Host to ssh to. Environment: \fBMU2EDAQ_TUNNEL_HOST\fR. Default:
\fImu2e\-dcs\-01.fnal.gov\fR.
.TP
.BR \-J ", " \-\-proxy\-jump " " \fIHOST\fR
ProxyJump host, or \fBnone\fR to connect directly. Environment:
\fBMU2EDAQ_TUNNEL_JUMP\fR. Default: \fImu2egateway02.fnal.gov\fR.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-state " " \fIFILE\fR
Where the ssh pids are recorded. Environment:
\fBMU2EDAQ_TUNNEL_STATE_SH\fR. Default: \fI~/.mu2edaq/daq_tunnels\fR.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-all\-matching
On \fBstop\fR, additionally \fBpkill\fR any ssh process matching the
Mu2e tunnel patterns. This can kill unrelated ssh sessions; use it only
when the state file has been lost.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt on \fBstop\fR.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the ssh command lines and change nothing.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_TUNNEL_SERVICES
Replaces the \fBdaq\fR service table.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_TUNNEL_EXTRA_SERVICES
Replaces the \fBextra\fR service table.
.TP
.B DAQ_TUNNELS_SH_OPTS
Extra options, prepended to the command line.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.mu2edaq/daq_tunnels
Recorded ssh pids, one \fBpid, group, host, offset\fR record per line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 when the action completed or the operator declined at the
prompt, 1 when there is no Kerberos ticket or the offset is not a
number, and 2 for an unrecognised option or argument. A failed
forwarding group is reported on stderr but does not by itself change
the exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open the standard forwards:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kinit
daq-tunnels.sh start
.fi
.RE
.PP
Open a second set 100 ports higher, so two operators do not collide:
.PP
.RS
.nf
daq-tunnels.sh -o 100 start
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what is currently forwarded:
.PP
.RS
.nf
daq-tunnels.sh status
.fi
.RE
.PP
Close them without being asked:
.PP
.RS
.nf
daq-tunnels.sh -y stop
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR daq-open-tunnels (1),
.BR start-novnc-connection.sh (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR kinit (1)
